WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.385
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level A two.

2
00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:09.304
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:15.437
Unit fifteen: Too / Enough. Today you will say that something is more than needed or sufficient.

4
00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:18.343
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.521
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:24.106
Too.

7
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:27.139
Too means more than is good or needed.

8
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.030
It is too hot.

9
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:31.630
Your turn. Say: Too.

10
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.294
Too.

11
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:36.796
Enough.

12
00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:39.748
Enough means as much as is needed.

13
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.537
I have enough money.

14
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.268
Your turn. Say: Enough.

15
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:47.960
Enough.

16
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:49.729
Too much.

17
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.715
Too much means an excessive amount of an uncountable noun.

18
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:55.782
There is too much sugar.

19
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:59.279
Your turn. Say: Too much.

20
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.042
Too much.

21
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:04.633
Too many.

22
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.729
Too many means an excessive number of countable nouns.

23
00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:10.759
There are too many people.

24
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.817
Your turn. Say: Too many.

25
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:17.494
Too many.

26
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.239
Not enough.

27
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.377
Not enough means less than is needed.

28
00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:24.507
We have not enough time.

29
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:27.757
Your turn. Say: Not enough.

30
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.485
Not enough.

31
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.181
Sufficient.

32
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:36.334
Sufficient means enough for a purpose.

33
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.462
The food is sufficient.

34
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.946
Your turn. Say: Sufficient.

35
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.766
Sufficient.

36
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:47.669
Excessive.

37
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:50.869
Excessive means more than is reasonable.

38
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:53.088
The noise is excessive.

39
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:56.440
Your turn. Say: Excessive.

40
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.475
Excessive.

41
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:02.798
Adjective + enough.

42
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:07.385
Adjective + enough means a pattern showing sufficient quality.

43
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:09.739
She is old enough to drive.

44
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.327
Your turn. Say: Adjective + enough.

45
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:17.888
Adjective + enough.

46
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.258
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:23.117 --> 00:02:26.350
Visal, is the tea hot? Can you drink it?

48
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:39.581
It is too hot. Too means more than is good, so it is a problem. I cannot drink it. Too goes before an adjective or adverb: too hot, too fast, too late.

49
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:41.650
And enough?

50
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:54.905
Enough means as much as we need. It goes after an adjective: She is old enough to drive. It goes before a noun: We have enough time. The noun pattern is enough + noun.

51
00:02:56.017 --> 00:02:57.912
What about too much and too many?

52
00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:10.507
Too much is for uncountable nouns: too much sugar. Too many is for countable nouns: too many people. Not enough means less than needed: not enough chairs.

53
00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:12.981
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:13.817 --> 00:03:15.851
Grammar focus: Too and enough.

55
00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:17.454
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.999
The tea is too hot to drink.

57
00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:22.604
She is old enough to drive.

58
00:03:23.517 --> 00:03:24.912
We have enough time.

59
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:35.339
Too + adjective = a problem. Adjective + enough = sufficient. Enough + noun. Use too much with uncountable nouns and too many with countable nouns.

60
00:03:36.083 --> 00:03:39.641
Be careful! Don't say: She is enough old to drive.

61
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.215
Say: She is old enough to drive.

62
00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:46.480
Put enough after the adjective.

63
00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:50.105
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.180
Too Much or Enough?.

65
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.466
The soup is too hot, so I cannot eat it now.

66
00:03:56.467 --> 00:04:00.010
There are too many people in the bus, and there is not enough space.

67
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.011
She is old enough to travel alone.

68
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.046
We have enough food for ten people.

69
00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:09.265
Too much noise makes it hard to study.

70
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:14.264
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:17.897
The bag is blank heavy. I can't lift it.

72
00:04:18.197 --> 00:04:21.293
A, enough. B, too. C, very enough.

73
00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:26.508
The answer is B: too.

74
00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:29.731
He is tall blank to touch the roof.

75
00:04:30.031 --> 00:04:32.964
A, too. B, enough. C, much.

76
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.662
The answer is B: enough.

77
00:04:39.817 --> 00:04:41.793
There is blank sugar in my tea.

78
00:04:42.093 --> 00:04:45.363
A, too many. B, too much. C, too.

79
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.969
The answer is B: too much.

80
00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:53.698
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:04:53.998 --> 00:04:58.801
A, She is enough old.. B, She is old enough.. C, She is too old enough..

82
00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.900
The answer is B: She is old enough..

83
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.002
According to the reading, what makes it hard to study?

84
00:05:09.302 --> 00:05:13.431
A, too much noise. B, enough food. C, a hot soup.

85
00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:19.512
The answer is A: too much noise.

86
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.258
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:27.627
Write one sentence with 'too' and one with 'enough'.

88
00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:38.068
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:38.368 --> 00:05:50.253
This bag is too heavy for me to carry. I am not strong enough to lift it. I use too before an adjective to show a problem, and I use enough after an adjective to show it is not sufficient.

90
00:05:50.553 --> 00:05:52.297
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:53.183 --> 00:05:56.962
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and too and enough.

92
00:05:57.262 --> 00:06:00.982
Today's value is attention. Look carefully and listen well.

93
00:06:01.282 --> 00:06:04.167
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:07.154
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

